38. { -KEY- } f5 $1 { The only way to keep material on the board. } exf5 ( 38... Bxe5 39. Qb7 $1 { Keeps the g-pawn. } ( 38... exf5 38. Qxa7 exf5 { and Black will reach an ending with rook and f-pawn versus queen. } ) 
Rd6 40. Qxa7 $1 ( 39... exf5 39. fxe6 $6 { Given in the first edition of the book. It allows Black to reach the same fortress as after 38...Bxe5! } ) 
exf5 41. gxf5 { Black must still give the bishop for the a-pawn, and with the white f-pawn on the board, there's no fortress. White wins. } ) 
 ( 38... Rxe5 $1 39. Qe7 $1 ( 38... exf5 38. Qd8 $2 { is a double threat against d4 and g5, but } Re3 40. Kg2 Bf6 $11 { defends. } ) 
Re3 40. Kg2 Bf6 41. Qxa7 Re4 $1 { Compared to 38...Bxe5, Black's rook is much better placed. } 42. fxe6 Rxe6 { Black will give the bishop for the a-pawn. White might also (by the help of zugzwang) win the g-pawn. It's a fortress anyway! If Black's king stays on g7, White can never sacrifice the queen to get a winning pawn ending. } ) 
39. e6 { A hard exercise. Objectively, 38. f5 only leads to a draw, but it's testing and probably rewarded in a game. } 
